<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="theme-color" content="#142d4c"><meta name="description" content="Explore assistance programs available in Michigan."><title>Michigan Assistance Programs — American Aid Network</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/site.css"><?php require __DIR__ . "/../../includes/google-analytics.php"; ?></head><body><?php require __DIR__ . "/../../includes/site-header.php"; ?><main><section class="state-page"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/help/">Browse Programs</a><span>›</span><b>Michigan</b></div><div class="state-intro"><div><h1>Assistance Programs in Michigan</h1><p>11 programs, grouped by what you need help with. Each includes clear application guidance.</p></div><div><a class="button primary" href="/find/">Find my programs <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><small>Free · Private · Takes under 2 minutes</small></div></div><section class="program-group"><h2>Cash Assistance &amp; Benefits</h2><div class="directory-cards"><article><h3>Family Independence Program (Michigan Cash Assistance)</h3><p>The Family Independence Program, FIP, is Michigan&#39;s TANF cash assistance for low-income families with children, paid monthly on the Bridge Card. Adults have a 60-month lifetime limit, and applicants take part in the PATH work program. Apply online through MI Bridges at newmibridges.michigan.gov or at your local MDHHS office.</p><div><a href="/programs/family-independence-program-michigan-cash-assistance/">How to apply <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/programs/family-independence-program-michigan-cash-assistance/">Program details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article><article><h3>Unemployment Benefits (Michigan)</h3><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Michigan Unemployment Insurance Agency. File online through MiWAM at michigan.gov/uia, which is the fastest way, or by phone at 1-866-500-0017. The customer service line is open Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm.</p><div><a href="/programs/unemployment-benefits-michigan/">How to apply <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/programs/unemployment-benefits-michigan/">Program details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article></div><a class="group-cta" href="/find/">Check what you qualify for <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></section><section class="program-group"><h2>Food Assistance</h2><div class="directory-cards"><article><h3>Food Assistance Program (Michigan SNAP)</h3><p>The Food Assistance Program, FAP, is Michigan&#39;s name for SNAP: monthly food money on a Bridge Card you use like a debit card at the store. Apply online through MI Bridges at newmibridges.michigan.gov, or with a paper application at your local MDHHS office.</p><div><a href="/programs/food-assistance-program-michigan-snap/">How to apply <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/programs/food-assistance-program-michigan-snap/">Program details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article><article><h3>Michigan WIC</h3><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children up to age 5. Michigan WIC is offered through county health departments and local agencies. Call 800-942-1636 to find your local clinic, or find one at Signupwic.com.</p><div><a href="/programs/michigan-wic/">How to apply <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/programs/michigan-wic/">Program details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article></div><a class="group-cta" href="/find/">Check what you qualify for <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></section><section class="program-group"><h2>Rent &amp; Housing Assistance</h2><div class="directory-cards"><article><h3>Michigan 211</h3><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Michigan phone, or call 844-875-9211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. Michigan 211 runs through seven regional call centers, covers the whole state, and answers around the clock. You can also text your zip code to 898211 or search at mi211.org.</p><div><a href="/programs/michigan-211/">How to apply <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/programs/michigan-211/">Program details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article><article><h3>Michigan Legal Help</h3><p>Michigan Legal Help is a free website with plain-language legal information and do-it-yourself tools that build court forms for people without a lawyer. It covers eviction, family law, debt, and public benefits, and points you to legal aid and self-help centers across the state. The tools and information are free to everyone.</p><div><a href="/programs/michigan-legal-help/">How to apply <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/programs/michigan-legal-help/">Program details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article><article><h3>Michigan Medicaid &amp; Healthy Michigan Plan</h3><p>Michigan Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. The Healthy Michigan Plan covers adults 19 to 64 who meet the income rules. Apply online through MI Bridges at newmibridges.michigan.gov, by phone at 1-855-789-5610, or at your local MDHHS office.</p><div><a href="/programs/michigan-medicaid-and-healthy-michigan-plan/">How to apply <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/programs/michigan-medicaid-and-healthy-michigan-plan/">Program details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article><article><h3>State Emergency Relief (Michigan SER)</h3><p>State Emergency Relief helps Michigan residents through a crisis that threatens health or safety: heat and electricity shut-offs, plus rent, past-due rent, security deposits, and moving costs. Energy help has no copay for eligible households. Apply online through MI Bridges at newmibridges.michigan.gov or with form DHS-1514 at your local MDHHS office.</p><div><a href="/programs/state-emergency-relief-michigan-ser/">How to apply <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/programs/state-emergency-relief-michigan-ser/">Program details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article></div><a class="group-cta" href="/find/">Check what you qualify for <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></section><section class="program-group"><h2>Debt &amp; Credit Help</h2><div class="directory-cards"><article><h3>Michigan Earned Income Tax Credit</h3><p>Michigan&#39;s Earned Income Tax Credit for Working Families is worth 30 percent of your federal EITC and is refundable, so it can put money in your pocket even if you owe no state tax. Public Act 4 of 2023 raised it from 6 percent to 30 percent. Claim it by filing a Michigan MI-1040 return after you qualify for the federal EITC.</p><div><a href="/programs/michigan-earned-income-tax-credit/">How to apply <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/programs/michigan-earned-income-tax-credit/">Program details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article></div><a class="group-cta" href="/find/">Check what you qualify for <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></section><section class="program-group"><h2>Utility Bill Help</h2><div class="directory-cards"><article><h3>Michigan Home Heating Credit</h3><p>The Home Heating Credit is a refundable Michigan tax credit that helps low-income residents pay winter heating costs. You can claim it even if you are not required to file a state income tax return, and you do not have to owe tax to get it. File form MI-1040CR-7 by September 30.</p><div><a href="/programs/michigan-home-heating-credit/">How to apply <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/programs/michigan-home-heating-credit/">Program details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article><article><h3>Weatherization Assistance Program (Michigan)</h3><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Michigan households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repairs that lower future bills. MDHHS runs the program through local Community Action Agencies. Contact the Community Action Agency serving your area to apply.</p><div><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-michigan/">How to apply <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-michigan/">Program details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article></div></section></div></section></main><footer><div class="wrap footer-grid"><div><a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="American Aid Network home"><span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true"><span>★</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span>AMERICAN <b>AID</b><small>NETWORK</small></span></a><p>Clear, reliable guidance to assistance programs across America.</p><div class="verified">✓ Information from trusted sources</div></div><div><h4>FIND SUPPORT</h4><a href="/find/">Find help near me</a><a href="/help/">Browse by state</a><a href="/programs/">Program guides</a><a href="/#how-it-works">How it works</a></div><div><h4>ABOUT</h4><a href="/about/">Our mission</a><a href="/legal/contact/">Contact us</a><a href="/legal/privacy/">Privacy policy</a><a href="/legal/terms/">Terms of use</a></div></div><div class="wrap disclaimer">American Aid Network is an independent informational resource. We are not a government agency and do not administer benefit programs. Program details change; always confirm requirements with the official provider.<span>© 2026 American Aid Network</span></div></footer><script src="/assets/site.js" defer></script></body></html>